  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":34,"t":"single_choice","p":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 70 customers at a mobile-money agent in Kumasi are grouped in the table. Calculate the frequency density of the class 20 ≤ t < 40, to 1 decimal place.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 70 customers at a mobile-money agent in Kumasi are grouped in the table. Calculate the frequency density of the class 20 ≤ t < 40, to 1 decimal place."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Waiting time t (minutes)"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequency f"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ t < 10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 ≤ t < 20"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20 ≤ t < 40"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40 ≤ t < 60"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total number of customers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"70"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width = 30 ÷ (40 − 20) = 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":34,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Frequency density = frequency ÷ width.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["1.5","30","3","0.7"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["The width of 20 ≤ t < 40 is 20, not 10.","Divide the frequency by the actual class width."]},"marking":""},{"n":35,"t":"single_choice","p":"The waiting times of 70 patients at a clinic in Dakar are grouped in classes of widths 10, 10, 20 and 30 minutes. A student draws the histogram shown here using the frequencies as the bar heights. Which statement correctly describes her graph?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The waiting times of 70 patients at a clinic in Dakar are grouped in classes of widths 10, 10, 20 and 30 minutes. A student draws the histogram shown here using the frequencies as the bar heights. Which statement correctly describes her graph?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q35_patients.jpg","alt":"The student’s histogram using frequencies as heights","width":96}}]},"e":"Histogram rule: with unequal class widths, bar area must be proportional to frequency, so bar height = frequency ÷ class width (frequency density). Her bars of heights 8, 20, 30, 12 would give areas 80, 200, 600, 360 instead of 8, 20, 30, 12 — the wide classes are wildly overstated.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":35,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Area — not height — must be proportional to frequency.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["With unequal class widths the heights must be frequency densities, because it is the area of each bar, not its height, that represents the frequency.","The heights should be frequency multiplied by class width, so the widest bar is the tallest.","The graph is correct, because a histogram may use frequencies as heights as long as the classes are in increasing order.","The bars should be reordered from the widest class to the narrowest class, with no gaps between them."],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Check what the area of her widest bar would represent.","Frequency density = frequency ÷ width; area = frequency."],"figure":"myp4_q35_patients"},"marking":""},{"n":36,"t":"single_choice","p":"The histogram shows the daily travel times, in minutes, of 40 delivery motorbike riders in Abidjan. Describe the shape of this distribution.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The histogram shows the daily travel times, in minutes, of 40 delivery motorbike riders in Abidjan. Describe the shape of this distribution."}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q36_riders.jpg","alt":"Histogram of daily travel times of 40 riders","width":96}}]},"e":"Bar heights: 4, 9, 13, 8, 4, 2 — a single clear mode in the 30–40 minute class, with frequencies trailing away towards the longer times (40–70). One peak plus a longer right tail means positive skew.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":36,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Find the peak, then see which side has the longer tail.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Skewed left (negatively skewed): unimodal, with a tail stretching towards the shorter times.","Symmetric and unimodal: the two tails are of about equal length, so the peak is in the middle.","Bimodal: two classes have frequencies as high as 9 and 13.","Skewed right (positively skewed): unimodal, with a tail stretching towards the longer times."],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Which class has the highest bar?","Does the tail stretch towards small or large values?"],"figure":"myp4_q36_riders"},"marking":""},{"n":37,"t":"single_choice","p":"Four histograms show the number of loaves of bread sold each morning over several mornings at four bakeries in Cotonou. All the classes have the same width. Which statement correctly describes the shapes?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four histograms show the number of loaves of bread sold each morning over several mornings at four bakeries in Cotonou. All the classes have the same width. Which statement correctly describes the shapes?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q37_bakeries.jpg","alt":"Histograms of daily loaves sold at bakeries P, Q, R and S","width":100}}]},"e":"P rises and falls evenly around the 50–55 class — symmetric. Q is tallest on the left (40–45) and tails off to the right (60–65) — skewed right. R has two high classes at the two ends with a dip in the middle — bimodal. S is tallest on the right (60–65) with frequencies climbing from 40 — skewed left.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":37,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"One peak or two? Which side is the tail on?","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["P is symmetric about its centre, Q is skewed right, R is bimodal and S is skewed left.","Q and S are both symmetric about 50 loaves, and R is skewed right.","All four distributions are unimodal and have the same modal class.","P is symmetric about its centre and Q is skewed left, with the tail towards the smaller numbers."],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Check P first — does it mirror itself?","R dips in the middle; S climbs towards the right edge."],"figure":"myp4_q37_bakeries"},"marking":""},{"n":38,"t":"single_choice","p":"The histogram shows the frequency density of the times, in minutes, that 70 customers spent at a bank counter in Rabat. How many customers spent between 30 and 45 minutes at the counter?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The histogram shows the frequency density of the times, in minutes, that 70 customers spent at a bank counter in Rabat. How many customers spent between 30 and 45 minutes at the counter?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q38_bank.jpg","alt":"Frequency-density histogram of times at a bank counter","width":96}}]},"e":"Frequency = area of the bar. The 30–45 class has density 0.8 and width 15, so frequency = 0.8 × 15 = 12 customers. (Check the rest: 0–5 → 3 × 5 = 15; 5–20 → 1.2 × 15 = 18; 20–30 → 2.5 × 10 = 25; 15 + 18 + 25 + 12 = 70 ✓.)\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Histograms and Distribution Shape","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":38,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"With density on the y-axis, frequency = density × width.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["0.8 customers","12 customers","18 customers","8 customers"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["The y-axis is density, not frequency.","Area = density × class width; reverse the same rule for this bar."],"figure":"myp4_q38_bank"},"marking":""},{"n":39,"t":"single_choice","p":"The times, in seconds, of 5 athletes in a 100 m sprint were as shown. Calculate the standard deviation of these times, to 2 decimal places, using the population formula σ = √(Σ(x - x̄)2 ÷ n).\\n\\nTimes in seconds: 43, 46, 41, 52, 48","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The times, in seconds, of 5 athletes in a 100 m sprint were as shown. Calculate the standard deviation of these times, to 2 decimal places, using the population formula σ = √(Σ(x - x̄)2 ÷ n)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Times in seconds: 43, 46, 41, 52, 48"}]}]},"e":"Mean x̄ = (43 + 46 + 41 + 52 + 48) ÷ 5 = 230 ÷ 5 = 46 s. Deviations: −3, 0, −5, 6, 2. Squared: 9, 0, 25, 36, 4 → Σ = 74. Variance = 74 ÷ 5 = 14.8. σ = √14.8 ≈ 3.85 s.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":39,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Find the mean, then the squared deviations.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["46","4.30","3.85","14.8"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["The mean of the five times is exactly 46.","Deviate from the mean, square, sum, divide by 5, then root."]},"marking":""},{"n":40,"t":"single_choice","p":"In a class test the mean score of 10 students was 62 and the standard deviation was 8. The teacher then adds 5 marks to every student’s score. What are the new mean and standard deviation?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a class test the mean score of 10 students was 62 and the standard deviation was 8. The teacher then adds 5 marks to every student’s score. What are the new mean and standard deviation?"}]}]},"e":"Adding a constant to every value shifts the mean by that constant (62 + 5 = 67) and leaves the spread unchanged — deviations from the mean are identical — so σ stays 8.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":40,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Adding a constant moves the mean, never the spread.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["67 and 40","67 and 13","62 and 8","67 and 8"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["What is 62 + 5?","Does adding 5 change how far scores sit from one another?"]},"marking":""},{"n":41,"t":"single_choice","p":"The table shows the number of loaves of bread sold each hour by a bakery in Porto-Novo over 30 hours. Calculate the standard deviation of the number of loaves sold per hour, to 2 decimal places.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table shows the number of loaves of bread sold each hour by a bakery in Porto-Novo over 30 hours. Calculate the standard deviation of the number of loaves sold per hour, to 2 decimal places."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loaves sold x"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Number of hours f"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"14"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"16"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"18"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Totals"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Mean = Σfx ÷ Σf = (10×4 + 12×7 + 14×9 + 16×7 + 18×3) ÷ 30 = (40 + 84 + 126 + 112 + 54) ÷ 30 = 416 ÷ 30 ≈ 13.87 loaves. Σfx² = 100×4 + 144×7 + 196×9 + 256×7 + 324×3 = 400 + 1008 + 1764 + 1792 + 972 = 5936. σ = √(Σfx² ÷ n − x̄²) = √(5936 ÷ 30 − 13.867²) = √(197.87 − 192.27) = √5.60 ≈ 2.36 loaves.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":41,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Use σ = √(Σfx² ÷ n − x̄²).","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["2.36 loaves","13.87 loaves","2.40 loaves","5.58 loaves"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Compute the mean from the frequency table first.","Apply the shortcut formula σ = √(Σfx² ÷ n − x̄²)."]},"marking":""},{"n":42,"t":"single_choice","p":"The variance of the monthly salaries, in FCFA, of the 40 employees of a company in Cotonou is 2 500 000. What is the standard deviation, to the nearest FCFA, and what does it measure?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The variance of the monthly salaries, in FCFA, of the 40 employees of a company in Cotonou is 2 500 000. What is the standard deviation, to the nearest FCFA, and what does it measure?"}]}]},"e":"σ = √variance = √2 500 000 ≈ 1581.14 ≈ 1581 FCFA (to the nearest FCFA). Variance is in FCFA², so taking the square root returns the original units — σ measures the spread (typical deviation) of salaries in FCFA.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":42,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"σ = √variance, and watch the units.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["62 500 FCFA — the variance divided by the 40 employees.","2 500 000 FCFA — the variance is already the standard deviation.","1 250 000 FCFA — half of the variance.","1 581 FCFA — σ is the square root of the variance, so it is measured in FCFA, like the salaries themselves."],"option_key":[4],"hints":["The variance is 2 500 000 — what is its square root?","What units does a square root of FCFA² carry?"]},"marking":""},{"n":43,"t":"single_choice","p":"The temperature x, in °C, was recorded every hour in Niamey. For the 24 readings the mean was 25 and the standard deviation was 6. Each reading is converted to degrees Fahrenheit by y = 1.8x + 32. What are the mean and standard deviation of the 24 values of y?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The temperature x, in °C, was recorded every hour in Niamey. For the 24 readings the mean was 25 and the standard deviation was 6. Each reading is converted to degrees Fahrenheit by y = 1.8x + 32. What are the mean and standard deviation of the 24 values of y?"}]}]},"e":"Linear transformation y = ax + b: the mean scales and shifts — x̄_y = 1.8 × 25 + 32 = 77. The standard deviation only scales: σ_y = |a| × σ_x = 1.8 × 6 = 10.8. The shift (+32) affects the centre, never the spread.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":43,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"For y = ax + b: mean → a·mean + b; σ → |a|·σ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["77 and 10.8","77 and 38","45 and 10.8","77 and 6"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Apply 1.8 × 25 + 32 to the mean.","Only the 1.8 stretches the spread — the + 32 slides every value together."]},"marking":""},{"n":44,"t":"single_choice","p":"A technician in Lagos recorded the number of phones he repaired each day for 40 days, and the table gives the totals Σfx = 154 and Σfx2 = 650. Use σ = √(Σfx2 ÷ n − x̄2) to calculate the standard deviation, to 2 decimal places.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A technician in Lagos recorded the number of phones he repaired each day for 40 days, and the table gives the totals Σfx = 154 and Σfx2 = 650. Use σ = √(Σfx2 ÷ n − x̄2) to calculate the standard deviation, to 2 decimal places."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Phones repaired x"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Number of days f"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Product fx"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Product fx2"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"90"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"48"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"192"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"200"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"144"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Totals"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"154"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"650"}]}]}]}]},"e":"x̄ = 154 ÷ 40 = 3.85 phones. Σfx² ÷ n = 650 ÷ 40 = 16.25. σ = √(16.25 − 3.85²) = √(16.25 − 14.8225) = √1.4275 ≈ 1.1948 ≈ 1.19 phones. (Check: Σfx = 12 + 30 + 48 + 40 + 24 = 154 ✓; Σfx² = 24 + 90 + 192 + 200 + 144 = 650 ✓.)\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":44,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"x̄ = 154 ÷ 40 first.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["1.19 phones","4.03 phones","1.21 phones","1.43 phones"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Compute the mean from the frequency table first.","Apply the shortcut formula σ = √(Σfx² ÷ n − x̄²)."]},"marking":""},{"n":45,"t":"single_choice","p":"A distribution is approximately normal. Which of the following statements is NOT true of it?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A distribution is approximately normal. Which of the following statements is NOT true of it?"}]}]},"e":"Normal facts: symmetry about μ ✓; mean = median = mode ✓; total area = 1 ✓. The 68–95–99.7 rule says about 68% of values lie within one σ, and about 95% lie within two σ — so ‘95% within one σ’ is the false statement.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":45,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Recall the 68–95–99.7 rule.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The curve is symmetric about the mean μ.","About 95% of the values lie within one standard deviation of the mean.","The mean, the median and the mode all have the same value.","The total area under the curve is 1, so the proportion of values in an interval is the area above that interval."],"option_key":[2],"hints":["What percentage of values lies within one σ of μ?","About 95% lies within two standard deviations, not one."]},"marking":""},{"n":46,"t":"single_choice","p":"The heights of maize stalks in a field are approximately normally distributed with mean 120 cm and standard deviation 15 cm, as shown. One stalk measures 141 cm. Calculate its z-score, to 2 decimal places.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The heights of maize stalks in a field are approximately normally distributed with mean 120 cm and standard deviation 15 cm, as shown. One stalk measures 141 cm. Calculate its z-score, to 2 decimal places."}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q46_maize.jpg","alt":"Normal curve for maize stalk heights with 141 cm marked","width":96}}]},"e":"z = (x − μ) ÷ σ = (141 − 120) ÷ 15 = 21 ÷ 15 = 1.4 (to 2 decimal places, 1.40). The stalk sits 1.4 standard deviations above the mean.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":46,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"z = (x − μ) ÷ σ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["1.40","−1.40","9.40","1.05"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["x = 141, μ = 120, σ = 15.","Subtract the mean from 141, then divide by 15."],"figure":"myp4_q46_maize"},"marking":""},{"n":47,"t":"single_choice","p":"The masses of eggs laid by a flock of hens in Ho are approximately normal with mean 60 g and standard deviation 4 g, as shown. What percentage of the eggs have a mass between 52 g and 56 g?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The masses of eggs laid by a flock of hens in Ho are approximately normal with mean 60 g and standard deviation 4 g, as shown. What percentage of the eggs have a mass between 52 g and 56 g?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q47_eggs.jpg","alt":"Normal curve for egg masses with the 52–56 g band shaded","width":96}}]},"e":"52 g is μ − 2σ and 56 g is μ − 1σ. By the 68–95–99.7 rule, 95% of values lie within 2σ and 68% within 1σ, so the shaded band between −2σ and −1σ holds (95 − 68) ÷ 2 = 13.5% of eggs.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":47,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Use symmetry of the 68–95–99.7 rule.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["47.5%","13.5%","16%","34%"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["52 = μ − 2σ and 56 = μ − 1σ.","Each outer band equals (95% − 68%) on either side, split equally."],"figure":"myp4_q47_eggs"},"marking":""},{"n":48,"t":"single_choice","p":"The time customers spend shopping in a supermarket in Accra is approximately normal with mean 25 minutes and standard deviation 5 minutes. For 95% of shoppers, between which two times is their shopping time?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The time customers spend shopping in a supermarket in Accra is approximately normal with mean 25 minutes and standard deviation 5 minutes. For 95% of shoppers, between which two times is their shopping time?"}]}]},"e":"95% of values lie within two standard deviations of the mean: μ ± 2σ = 25 ± 10 minutes → 15 to 35 minutes.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":48,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"95% means ± 2σ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["10 min and 40 min","20 min and 30 min","15 min and 35 min","25 min and 35 min"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["μ = 25 and σ = 5 — work out 25 ± 2 × 5.","Evaluate both ends of the interval — one is the lower limit, one is the upper limit."]},"marking":""},{"n":49,"t":"single_choice","p":"In a mathematics contest the 60 scores in the Nairobi branch have mean 84 and standard deviation 6, while the 80 scores in the Kampala branch have mean 90 and standard deviation 10. Amina scored 90 in Nairobi and Otieno scored 90 in Kampala. Who did better relative to their own branch?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a mathematics contest the 60 scores in the Nairobi branch have mean 84 and standard deviation 6, while the 80 scores in the Kampala branch have mean 90 and standard deviation 10. Amina scored 90 in Nairobi and Otieno scored 90 in Kampala. Who did better relative to their own branch?"}]}]},"e":"Amina: z = (90 − 84) ÷ 6 = 1.00 — one standard deviation above her branch mean. Otieno: z = (90 − 90) ÷ 10 = 0.00 — exactly at his branch mean. Relative to their own branches, Amina performed better. Different branch statistics do not prevent comparison; the z-scores normalise them.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":49,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Standardise both scores with z = (x − μ) ÷ σ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Otieno did better for his own branch: his z-score is 1.00, against Amina’s z-score of 0.00.","Amina did better for her own branch: her z-score is 1.00, against Otieno’s z-score of 0.00.","They did equally well, because both scored the raw mark of 90.","No comparison is possible, because the two branches have different means and standard deviations."],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Compute each z-score: (score − branch mean) ÷ branch σ.","The bigger z-score did better relative to their branch."]},"marking":""},{"n":50,"t":"single_choice","p":"The lifetime of a torch battery is approximately normal with mean 200 hours and standard deviation 20 hours, as shown. The maker replaces free of charge any battery that fails before 160 hours. In a batch of 1000 batteries, how many are expected to be replaced?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lifetime of a torch battery is approximately normal with mean 200 hours and standard deviation 20 hours, as shown. The maker replaces free of charge any battery that fails before 160 hours. In a batch of 1000 batteries, how many are expected to be replaced?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp4/q50_batteries.jpg","alt":"Normal curve for battery lifetimes with the tail below 160 hours shaded","width":96}}]},"e":"160 hours is μ − 2σ. The left tail below μ − 2σ holds (100 − 95) ÷ 2 = 2.5% of batteries (68–95–99.7 rule, using two σ). 2.5% of 1000 = 25 batteries expected to be replaced. The paper does not permit normal tables; 2.5% is all the information given.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":50,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"160 = μ − 2σ; the far-left tail is 2.5%.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["250 batteries","50 batteries","25 batteries","950 batteries"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["(100% − 95%) ÷ 2 = 2.5% of the distribution lies below 160 hours.","Apply that tail percentage to the 1000 batteries manufactured."],"figure":"myp4_q50_batteries"},"marking":""}]'::jsonb) as x
